{
 "id": "l04",
 "label": "4",
 "title": "The articles si and ang",
 "summary": "The little words that go before the subject, and how to show more than one",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Use si, siná, ang and ang mga before the subject of a sentence",
  "Build simple sentences with ay (subject first, predicate last)",
  "Show plurals with siná, mga and plural adjectives",
  "Use ang before names of places"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Every subject needs an article",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Any noun used as the **subject** of a sentence, including names of persons, places and things, is always preceded by an **article**. The only exceptions are **direct conversation** (talking straight to someone) and **imperative statements** (commands)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Article",
      "Used before",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[si|marker]]",
       "the name of one person",
       "**Si** Ruth"
      ],
      [
       "[[siná|marker]]",
       "the names of two or more persons",
       "**Sina** Helen at Tom"
      ],
      [
       "[[ang|marker]]",
       "the name of a single thing or place",
       "**Ang** batà, **Ang** áso"
      ],
      [
       "[[ang mga|marker]]",
       "the names of two or more things or places",
       "**Ang mga** batà, **Ang mga** áso"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The four articles"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Subject first, predicate last",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Pattern",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Si",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "Ruth",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ay",
         "r": "ay"
        },
        {
         "t": "babáe",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Article + subject + ay + predicate. Ay joins the subject to the predicate."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Compare with English",
     "t": "English has articles too: *the* and *a*. In Tagalog the article is needed even before a person's name, and there are different ones for one person, several persons, one thing and several things."
    }
   ]
  },
